WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:02.940
SPEAKER 1: Oceanic
ecosystems are

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.070
threatened by a lack of
science-based fishery

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.330
management today.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:11.100
For example, 34% of the world's
major commercial fish species

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.420
are overfished.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.570
And more than $23
billion of seafood

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:20.040
is stolen annually from the sea
through illegal and unregulated

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.250
fishing, also causing
habitat destruction.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.820
This is why a free website
called GlobalFishingWatch.org

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.560
was launched by
Google in partnership

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.880
with other organizations,
such as Oceana and SkyTruth,

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.470
in 2016.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.960
To monitor fishing
activity globally

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.320
and make it transparent
to anyone online for free.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.320
They use satellite technology,
cloud computing, and machine

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.990
learning in order to
find navigation patterns,

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:50.040
using a public signal received
by a GPS-like device called

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.880
the Automatic Identification
System or AIS, used

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.680
by the vessels in the sea.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.620
You can imagine tracking 60
million points from vessels

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.230
worldwide quickly grows
into a big data problem.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.905
So let's dive in and see
how to approach the problem.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.130
Thanks to data, today we can
understand our natural world

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:15.690
with far greater
richness than ever

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.510
before by orders of magnitude.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.540
This now empowers us to
build amazing apps that

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.780
can help us solve complex social
and environmental challenges.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.260
And so in this series,
let's build an app together.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.800
Meet Brian, who's worked at
Google for over 14 years.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.603
He's a co-founder of
Global Fishing Watch.

00:01:34.603 --> 00:01:37.020
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So to understand
why we did Global Fishing

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.210
Watch, we first really
had to understand

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.470
what the state of fishing at a
global level was in the world.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.850
And the stats were
pretty sobering.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.540
So when you think of something
like a tuna or a swordfish,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.300
about 90% of that
species has disappeared

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.430
in the last generation or two.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:57.000
And when you look at fish that
actually end up on a plate,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.090
you're talking about how about
one in five, or 20% of those,

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:05.628
is considered IUU, so Illegal,
Unreported, or Unregulated.

00:02:05.628 --> 00:02:07.920
And then when you finally
look at fisheries themselves,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.620
they say that about
90% plus of them

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.470
were either at
capacity, overfished,

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.520
or trying to recover.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.880
So when we started
Global Fishing Watch,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.900
I like to say that it wasn't
possible three years earlier.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.510
It was barely feasible
when Global Fishing Watch

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.380
was starting.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.040
And now we wonder how
we didn't do it sooner.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.100
And from a pure technology and
machine learning perspective,

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.280
our first versions of this
were rough heuristics.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.950
It gave us a rough sense
of where a vessel might be.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.920
And now machine learning
has evolved so far so

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.870
that we're able to not
only see whether or not

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.450
a vessel's fishing based
on how it's moving.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.240
We're also able to differentiate
the type of fishing

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.570
that they're doing.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.620
And so we continue to stay on
the cutting edge of machine

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.390
learning as it applies to the
fundamental conservation issues

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.617
or policy issues that we're
addressing with fishing.

00:02:59.617 --> 00:03:01.950
SPEAKER 1: I love this project,
and definitely recommend

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.190
leveraging all the extensive
work the amazing Global Fishing

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.710
Watch teams have made
accessible on their website

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.475
as well as on their
beautiful YouTube channel.

00:03:10.475 --> 00:03:11.850
BRIAN SULLIVAN:
So within Google,

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.620
I feel really lucky
that I get to work

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.570
on a team whose focus is to make
a positive change in the world

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:20.700
from an environmental
or societal perspective.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.320
I honestly don't
think this would

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.210
have been possible
without Google

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.640
willing to invest in
something like fishing,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.550
that isn't something you would
normally associate with Google.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.310
At the end of the day,
it was about making

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.520
information universally
accessible and digestible,

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:36.940
very much part of our mission.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.850
So to actually build
Global Fishing Watch,

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:43.080
we really used the full suite of
Google Cloud platform products.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.020
Fundamentally, BigQuery
was the only tool

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.670
that was out there
that was enabling

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:50.460
us to look at billions and
billions of these ship position

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:51.390
reports.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:54.840
And be able to look and them
not only over time, but also

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:55.710
spatially.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.590
Just storing that alone
was the type of thing

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.050
that didn't scale to
a lot of other areas.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.690
But then the ability
to go in and analyze it

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.720
in near real time, so
it enables an analyst

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.820
to kind of see how
this is unfolding

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.430
and tweak their
algorithms as necessary.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.670
SPEAKER 1: And if you're curious
about how to make a time series

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:19.329
classification app that includes
latitude and longitudinal data,

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:23.680
let me give you a quick tour
using Google Cloud tools.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.580
Let's start with a snapshot
of the products used.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.820
First up is Apache Beam, which
is an open source library

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.550
that you run a Google's data
processing service, called

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.310
Data Flow, to create
two data sets needed

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:36.700
for machine learning.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.680
One is for training and
the other for evaluation.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.460
Next is TensorFlow Keras,
which is a high level API

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.100
library used to define a
machine learning model,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.490
where we then
train in Vertex AI.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.190
As for the data, we get this
from Global Fishing Watch's

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.900
GitHub repository.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.360
It includes a ship's
unique identifier

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.800
called Maritime Mobile Service
Identity or MMSI, which

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.370
was broadcasted at different
times along with its lat,

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.070
long coordinates,
distance from shore

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.430
and port, its speed,
and its course

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.170
at different times of the day.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:17.430
The irregularity in time in
which the data's collected then

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.600
requires us to transform it into
even hourly intervals, which

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.310
we call time steps.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.380
The model uses a
one-dimensional,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.260
Fully Convolutional
Network, FCN.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.650
Because it enables us to feed a
variable number of hourly time

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:32.490
steps.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.890
It assesses patterns in
the vessel's movements

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:36.810
within each window of time.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:40.290
And as an output, it labels
whether it is fishing or not.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.360
Now, for this type of
supervised learning,

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.120
we need training data
that has fishing labels.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.490
And Global Fishing Watch
performed the labor

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.890
of love of manually
investigating and then

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:53.640
producing over 140,000
label time ranges.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.740
And for context,
even though that's

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.680
a tiny subset relative
to the massive data set,

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.440
it's more than enough
to build a model.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.540
Next, we create the
training and evaluation

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.030
data sets in parallel
via an Apache Beam

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.860
pipeline running on Data Flow.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.350
Now, from a data
preparation standpoint,

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:13.290
we ideally want to feed
the model raw data that

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.420
resembles what we
get from the field

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.660
in order to get predictions
as new data arrives.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.060
Next, we define the
model using Keras,

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:25.590
and train it on Vertex AI, which
is an API with a client library

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:30.030
and user interface that enables
you to train, save, deploy,

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.070
and request predictions.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.890
After our model is trained,
we save it in Cloud Storage

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:37.800
and can either load it
into a Python app, deploy

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.610
it as an endpoint in Vertex AI,
or in a web app using Cloud Run

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:43.530
Serverless infrastructure.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.900
We visualize the predictions
in a free Jupyter

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.220
notebook called Colab using
a map visualization library.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:53.100
We can see the ship
moving and changing color

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.690
when it's fishing
and when it's not,

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:55.898
according to the predictions.

00:06:55.898 --> 00:06:57.525
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:02.510
So what's next?

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:05.260
Well, in this video description
is a detailed blog post,

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.970
some friendly links,
and most importantly,

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.840
an awesome lab built in
collaboration with my peer

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.900
David, which contains all
the code needed so you do not

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.720
have to write a single line
and can interact with the map

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:17.530
yourself.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:20.270
If you found this content
helpful, by the way,

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:23.005
you can subscribe or leave
a comment on other AI

00:07:23.005 --> 00:07:25.270
for Good use cases
you have in mind.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:27.317
And community,
before we end, I'm

00:07:27.317 --> 00:07:29.775
going to leave you with Brian's
fun fact about the project.

00:07:29.775 --> 00:07:32.890
BRIAN SULLIVAN: It's really
shaped kind of the way I go out

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:36.100
and the way I interact
with nature now.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.160
And it just makes me really
kind of figure out what's around

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:40.900
the corner for the next thing.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.450
And there's so many
incredible stories

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:46.030
of seeing what happens when
you simply put information

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:49.180
in the hands of others, and
how they go on and take that

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:50.200
into other areas.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:52.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

